{"parentUuid":"9eab9565-d663-4d08-810c-27f598f68cc6","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgMQa7smbG8ruUh6aUyof","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"tool_use","id":"toolu_01LzL6yNpY6vqziWpYHdWua6","name":"Agent","input":{"description":"Adversarial: test assurance","subagent_type":"bug-hunter","prompt":"You are a principal engineer doing an ADVERSARIAL review of the VERIFICATION of \"Callisto\", a Rust CLI + GitHub Action that versions and releases polyglot workspaces (Cargo/npm/PyPI). Repo: /Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.claude/worktrees/smoke-main (main, v0.14.2). Read AGENTS.md, ARCHITECTURE.md, justfile, .github/workflows/callisto-ci.yml, docs/specs/INDEX.md first. Product goal: users trust it with irreversible public-registry publishes, so the question is: if a bug exists, would our tests, CI and specs catch it, or would they give false assurance?\n\nRULES: read-only. Do NOT edit/commit/push, do NOT run cargo build/test/clippy/publish, no network. You may read, grep, count, and run the prebuilt binary /Users/gabe/.claude/jobs/15c732d1/tmp/profile-target/release/callisto on scratch repos under a UNIQUE directory `$TMPDIR/adv-tests-$$` (other agents run in parallel; never share paths; never rm outside your own dir).\n\nYOUR LENS: test and CI assurance. Investigate: (1) what the release-path tests actually exercise vs fake: the fake `cargo`/`npm`/`gh`/`git` runners in tests and in crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/release_simulator.rs and release_test_support.rs — which production behaviors can the fakes NOT reproduce (auth, output formats of real tools, exit codes, partial failures, version-skew of npm/cargo/pnpm/twine), so a regression would pass; (2) which crucial invariants lack a failing-case test (find 8-10 important behaviors and for each check whether a test would FAIL if the behavior were inverted or removed: e.g. redaction, E320 refusal, tag-on-wrong-commit refusal, clean-worktree check, intent digest binding, token not in plan/build jobs, confirmation-window bound, unsafe range rewrite refusal, atomic writes, path containment); (3) snapshot tests (insta) that pin whatever the code does without anyone reviewing meaning — which are rubber stamps; (4) the coverage gate (90%) — what is excluded or gamed, which risky modules sit near the floor (use the structure of the repo and any coverage config; do not run coverage); (5) the CI workflow itself: jobs that can pass while testing nothing (skips, `continue-on-error`, conditional steps, tools missing -> silent skip), required checks vs the branch ruleset, actions pinned vs floating, flaky-test risk (sleeps, ports, real time), tests that touch the network or global git config; (6) property/fuzz coverage (crates/*/fuzz, proptest) for parsers and editors; (7) spec/code drift mechanisms: is there any automated check tying docs/specs/*.json criteria to tests? (8) .github/tests/*.sh workflow contract tests: what do they really assert.\nFor each finding give: severity (high = a likely real bug class would not be caught), concrete evidence (file:line), the specific regression that would slip through, and a one-line recommendation of the single cheapest test/CI change that closes it. Rank. At most 12 findings; no speculation without evidence. Also list 3-5 verification practices that are STRONG. Be concise and precise."},"caller":{"type":"direct"}}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"tool_use","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":532143,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"output_tokens":6842,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":784},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":532143,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":6842,"cache_read_input_tokens":0,"cache_creation_input_tokens":532143,"cache_creation":{"ephem