r.\n     - Evidence: a second `callisto version` after an uncommitted first run fails with E121 \"this looks like an interrupted version run\". That diagnosis is wrong for the common case of not having committed yet.\n     - Evidence: `callisto release` before committing gives E267 (\"found Cargo.lock\").\n     - Evidence: docs/publishing.md has no first-publish guidance (grep for \"first/bootstrap/trusted\" finds only a 14-day PyPI note). crates.io needs the crate to exist before trusted publishing, npm scoped packages need `--access`, and PyPI needs a pending publisher. All of that is outside the tool but inside the user's first hour.\n     - Evidence: the 10-minute goal is unmeasured (ROAD-TO-V1).\n     - Recommendation: time the path and publish the number. Give E121 a branch for \"complete run, not yet committed\". Add a \"first publish per registry\" section. Drop `id-token: write` until it is used.\n  \n  9. **[should-fix] The no-journal recovery depends on live registry observation, and the failure modes are the hard ones.**\n     - Evidence: confirmation windows are fixed by registry (`confirm-timeout` 6m for npm and PyPI, 2m for Cargo). npm can serve stale documents for up to 5 minutes (docs/config.md), so the Cargo and npm defaults already sit at or beyond the edge. E157 and E176 are \"indeterminate\" and the CLI prints a redacted last-failed-command trailer.\n     - Evidence: no production-path registry tests for Cargo and PyPI. Tests drive the publisher argv directly and bypass binding and observation, because `registry_endpoint.rs` rejects non-https loopback (ROAD-TO-V1).\n     - Harm: the one feature the tool sells (safe reruns) is unproven end-to-end on two of three registries.\n     - Recommendation: allow loopback http under a test-only seam and run the real observation path in CI. Do this before 1.0.\n  \n  10. **[consider] The release model's trust story is weaker for consumers than for this repo.**\n      - Evidence: ADR 2 says only `execute` gets registry secrets. The generated consumer workflow runs `mode: release` in one job with `CARGO_REGISTRY_TOKEN` in env. wire-compat.md says the generated workflow passes the intent and manifest between jobs \"without a checksum or comparison step, which this repository's own release workflow has\". The decision digest is an unkeyed SHA-256, not a signature (ADR 2).\n      - Harm: the safety properties demonstrated in the project's own repo are not what users get.\n      - Recommendation: either generate the three-job form with the checksum step, or say plainly that consumers get a simpler trust model.\n  \n  11. **[consider] The changeset format is compatible at the file level but not in package naming.**\n      - Evidence: changesets written by `callisto add` use `cargo/foo: minor` and `npm/foo: patch` in the front matter. `@changesets/cli` expects npm package names there.\n      - Harm: ADR 1 promises compatibility, but a Cargo or PyPI entry is unreadable to the original tool, and a migrating team cannot run both. Dual-name packages need the qualified form in files that `@changesets/cli` can no longer parse.\n      - Recommendation: state the compatibility claim precisely (\"npm-only workspaces round-trip; mixed workspaces use qualified keys\").\n  \n  12. **[consider] Competitor fit: scope against the field.**\n      - A team picks `changesets` for npm-only repos, `release-plz` for Cargo-only with conventional commits and registry diffing, `cargo-dist` for binary distribution and installers (Callisto only attaches binaries, with no installers or checksums yet), `semantic-release` for commit-driven single packages, and Nx release if it is already in the repo.\n      - Callisto wins on one narrow case: mixed Cargo+npm+PyPI repos that need a committed, auditable release roster. That case is real but small.\n      - Recommendation: lead the README with that case, and be explicit that there is no installer story. The repo already links competitors in docs/how-callisto-works.md; the 1.0 messaging should do the same.\n  \n  ## Decisions that are right and should be defended\n  \n  - **ADR 2: the committed decision is the release authority.** CI verifies the merged diff against the committed decision and never re-derives cascade or group policy after merge. `fixed_group_cascade_bump_without_direct_changeset_is_accepted` and the tamper tests back it. It removes a whole class of \"the plan changed between review and publish\" bugs.\n  - **ADR 3 and ADR 9: rerun by observing providers.** There is no execution journal, and an existing annotated tag on the planned commit is adopted whatever its message. State lives in the registries and Git, which is where it can be checked. Local and remote checks agree.\n  - **ADR 4: native tools publish and own authentication.** Callisto never holds registry credentials in its own client code, and the secrets boundary is small and auditable.\n  - **ADR 8 and invariant 2: format-preserving writes.** `toml_edit`, fingerprinted JSON, and `atomic_write` behind an `ApplyPermit` mean a version bump produces minimal diffs that reviewers can read.\n  - **Directory-keyed internal identity (ADR 11).** The directory key is the right stable internal key and the display id is the wrong thing to persist. The mistake to avoid is letting the display id leak into tags (finding 1), not the key itself.\n  \n  Smaller observations: `init` writes a `callisto.toml` containing only a comment, which is the right amount of ceremony. The generated workflow is small. Unknown-key rejection and E316/E317 messages are clear and actionable.\n  \n  All findings above are evidenced by file reads or the commands run. I did not find evidence that would let me rate the GitLab, Go or NuGet cases beyond the model-level coupling noted in finding 7.","handback":true,"handbackReportText":"ADVERSARIAL ARCHITECTURE / PRODUCT-FIT REVIEW — Callisto v0.14.2 (read-only; probes run with the prebuilt binary in /tmp/claude-501/adv-arch-38234 and adv-arch-wf-62357)\n\nDerived goal: a trustworthy, low-ceremony polyglot release tool. The pitch is a committed decision as release authority (ADR 2), registry-observed recovery with no journal (ADR 3), changesets compatibility (ADR 1), under 10 minutes to a first release (ROAD-TO-V1 says this is unmeasured).\n\nVerdict: the release-safety core is well designed. The 1.0 risks are the contract surface (identity, tag names, wire formats) and the amount of machinery behind it.\n\n## Findings, ranked\n\n1. **[blocks-1.0] The tag-name contract depends on workspace composition.**\n   - Evidence: ADR 11 and docs/architecture/identity.md. The default tag is `{display id}@{version}`, so a package is `foo@1.0.0` while its name is unique and becomes `cargo/foo@1.1.0` once any other ecosystem adds a package named `foo`. The old tags are found again through `history::sole_owner_at`, which uses `git grep` and manifest reads at each tag's commit.\n   - Probe: with cargo/foo, npm/foo and pypi/foo, `release --dry-run` plans three separate tags, all qualified.\n   - Harm: tag names are a public API. They feed `gh release download callisto@X`, the proto plugin, dependabot, and users' own scripts. An unrelated PR that adds a same-named package silently changes the namespace of future tags. Identity.md also admits renames and moves \"leave no evidence in Git\" and need a hand-written `previous-tag-templates`.\n   - Recommendation: for 1.0, make tags independent of composition. Either always qualify by default (`cargo/foo@v`) or persist the tag template at the first release. Do not carry ownership inference past 1.0.\n\n2. **[blocks-1.0] The ADR set contradicts the shipped contract.**\n   - Evidence: the ADR index and ADR 12 say \"Proposed... not accepted or implemented\". But ROAD-TO-V1 says canonical `ecosystem/name` \"shipped\", and the binary enforces it (`add --package foo:minor` gives E317, `cargo:foo` gives E316). identity.md also says `Bare` ids still exist internally (`PackageId::Bare`).\n   - Harm: the document that governs the identity contract describes the opposite of what runs. Anyone reading ADR 12 gets the wrong 1.0 story.\n   - Recommendation: accept ADR 12 and rewrite it as current behavior, with one internal-vs-user-facing identity section. Delete the stale \"proposed\" language.\n\n3. **[blocks-1.0] There is no stable, versioned machine contract, and the CI route depends on private shapes.**\n   - Evidence: docs/architecture/wire-compat.md lists its own gaps.\n     - Six release documents carry different schemaVersions (decision 2, intent 5, envelope 3, receipt 2, manifest 1, release-pr snapshot 2).\n     - The receipt version does not track its nested types.\n     - `callisto schema --type release-receipt` is the only published release schema, and its `schemaVersion` is an unconstrained integer.\n     - Shell `jq` readers (the workflow, run-release.sh) read `.artifactSlots` and `.operations[].id.package` with no version gate.\n     - Two readers \"tightened without a version bump\".\n     - The release-PR script hard-codes snapshot `schemaVersion: 2` while the action's `version` input can pin another binary.\n   - Evidence: the CI path is `release plan`, `release execute`, `release artifact-manifest`, `release-pr`, `matrix`, `compose-pr-body` and `schema`, all hidden from `--help` but required by generated and in-repo workflows. Hidden commands are still public API.\n   - Harm: version skew between action and binary fails at runtime, not at install. Users cannot tell what is covered by semver.\n   - Recommendation: declare a 1.0 surface. List which hidden commands and documents are supported and which are internal. Publish a schema for each supported document. Gate every reader on its version. Add the planned `cargo-semver-checks`/schema-diff CI check before 1.0, not after.\n\n4. **[should-fix] Exit codes and error taxonomy give automation too little to act on.**\n   - Evidence: the only exit codes in the CLI are `ExitCode::FAILURE` and `SUCCESS` (main.rs:94, version.rs:88). The code distinguishes \"indeterminate\" outcomes (E157, E176) from hard failures only to print a trailer line, not in the exit status.\n   - Evidence: the same bad input gets different codes in different commands. `add --package foo` returns E317, while `release --package foo` returns E227 with a redundant message (\"expected an ecosystem-qualified package id... use an exact ecosystem-qualified identity\").\n   - Evidence: an unknown config key reports E111 \"is not valid TOML\", which is not what happened.\n   - Harm: a CI wrapper cannot tell \"retry\" from \"stop\" from \"nothing to release\". Error codes are described as stable in docs/errors.md but are not consistent per condition.\n   - Recommendation: freeze a small exit-code set (ok, usage/config, retryable-indeterminate, conflict). Collapse duplicate diagnostics for the same condition before the codes are frozen. Rename E111 to a config error.\n\n5. **[should-fix] Release ownership for dual packages is derived by a hard-coded priority, and mixed outcomes are not in the contract.**\n   - Evidence: ADR 12 says the owner of the shared tag, forge release and announcement is \"the first release id by ecosystem priority Cargo, npm, PyPI\". `Ecosystem::primary_priority` hard-codes it. Co-located Cargo+Python fails with E007 (identity.md, ROAD-TO-V1).\n   - Harm: a same-directory npm+cargo package (napi-rs, the supported layout) can have different native names and versions. One tag and one release per package hides which distribution failed. A half-published pair (crates.io done, npm failed) has no named state in the user-facing contract. This priority list is also a one-way door once users' tag names depend on it.\n   - Recommendation: before 1.0, show a worked half-failure of a dual package in releasing.md. Make the owner an explicit config field with a derived default, or declare the priority permanent in an ADR.\n\n6. **[should-fix] The complexity budget is spent on recovery machinery, with no persisted state to anchor it.**\n   - Evidence: Rust source is about 8k lines in model, 8k in cli, 8k in manifests and up to 45k in graph (tests included). `commands/release/derive.rs` is 2,178 lines, `release_decision.rs` 1,632, `release_simulator.rs` 1,564, `provider/registry.rs` 1,222. identity.md describes `git grep` plus manifest reading to attribute historical tags, which is inference code on a path that should be a lookup.\n   - Evidence: dead and legacy surface in a pre-1.0 codebase.\n     - `Ecosystem` has Go, Maven, NuGet, Deno and Jsr variants in the MIT model crate.\n     - `publish-to` accepts `nuget` although \"accepting nuget does not provide a working publisher\" (docs/config.md).\n     - The `[init]` table is \"deprecated, never read\".\n     - `[release.profiles]` is legacy.\n     - The action input `setup_git_user` is a documented no-op.\n     - `matrix.rs` carries its own 18-triple table (ROAD-TO-V1).\n   - Harm: every legacy key becomes a 1.0 promise.\n   - Recommendation: delete `[init]`, `release.profiles`, `setup_git_user` and the unusable `nuget` publish target before tagging 1.0. Keep non-shipped ecosystems out of public enums, or gate them behind `#[non_exhaustive]` helpers (the enum is already `#[non_exhaustive]`).\n\n7. **[should-fix] Extensibility claims are ahead of the code (Go, NuGet, GitLab).**\n   - Evidence: ADR 12 and polyglot-v1.md say Go and NuGet are \"design examples, not shipped adapters\". Hosting is GitHub-only: `GitHubRepository` appears in model types (`release/operation.rs`, `release/artifact.rs`, `release/digest.rs`, `release/github.rs`, `release_pr.rs`) and so in the intent digest. `gh` is the forge client (`provider/policy.rs`). Release-PR actions are a GitHub composite action, and the in-repo workflow is 493 lines.\n   - Harm: adding GitLab is a wire-format change to the intent and digest, so it breaks the 1.0 compatibility promise. A new registry needs touches in `derive.rs`, `registry.rs`, the matrix and config, not one adapter.\n   - Recommendation: say \"GitHub plus Cargo/npm/PyPI\" in the 1.0 docs. Do not market extensibility. If a second forge is likely, put the forge host in the intent as a tagged enum now.\n\n8. **[should-fix] Onboarding is longer than the quick start suggests, and some first-hour steps are untested or undocumented.**\n   - Evidence: the README quick start uses `init --yes --versioning independent --no-workflow` and ends with a local `callisto release`. The real path is `--workflow`, which generates a 30-line workflow that uses `CARGO_REGISTRY_TOKEN` and grants `id-token: write` that is unused until OIDC lands post-1.0.\n   - Evidence: bare `init --yes` fails with E241 (`--versioning` required), which is clear.\n   - Evidence: a second `callisto version` after an uncommitted first run fails with E121 \"this looks like an interrupted version run\". That diagnosis is wrong for the common case of not having committed yet.\n   - Evidence: `callisto release` before committing gives E267 (\"found Cargo.lock\").\n   - Evidence: docs/publishing.md has no first-publish guidance (grep for \"first/bootstrap/trusted\" finds only a 14-day PyPI note). crates.io needs the crate to exist before trusted publishing, npm scoped packages need `--access`, and PyPI needs a pending publisher. All of that is outside the tool but inside the user's first hour.\n   - Evidence: the 10-minute goal is unmeasured (ROAD-TO-V1).\n   - Recommendation: time the path and publish the number. Give E121 a branch for \"complete run, not yet committed\". Add a \"first publish per registry\" section. Drop `id-token: write` until it is used.\n\n9. **[should-fix] The no-journal recovery depends on live registry observation, and the failure modes are the hard ones.**\n   - Evidence: confirmation windows are fixed by registry (`confirm-timeout` 6m for npm and PyPI, 2m for Cargo). npm can serve stale documents for up to 5 minutes (docs/config.md), so the Cargo and npm defaults already sit at or beyond the edge. E157 and E176 are \"indeterminate\" and the CLI prints a redacted last-failed-command trailer.\n   - Evidence: no production-path registry tests for Cargo and PyPI. Tests drive the publisher argv directly and bypass binding and observation, because `registry_endpoint.rs` rejects non-https loopback (ROAD-TO-V1).\n   - Harm: the one feature the tool sells (safe reruns) is unproven end-to-end on two of three registries.\n   - Recommendation: allow loopback http under a test-only seam and run the real observation path in CI. Do this before 1.0.\n\n10. **[consider] The release model's trust story is weaker for consumers than for this repo.**\n    - Evidence: ADR 2 says only `execute` gets registry secrets. The generated consumer workflow runs `mode: release` in one job with `CARGO_REGISTRY_TOKEN` in env. wire-compat.md says the generated workflow passes the intent and manifest between jobs \"without a checksum or comparison step, which this repository's own release workflow has\". The decision digest is an unkeyed SHA-256, not a signature (ADR 2).\n    - Harm: the safety properties demonstrated in the project's own repo are not what users get.\n    - Recommendation: either generate the three-job form with the checksum step, or say plainly that consumers get a simpler trust model.\n\n11. **[consider] The changeset format is compatible at the file level but not in package naming.**\n    - Evidence: changesets written by `callisto add` use `cargo/foo: minor` and `npm/foo: patch` in the front matter. `@changesets/cli` expects npm package names there.\n    - Harm: ADR 1 promises compatibility, but a Cargo or PyPI entry is unreadable to the original tool, and a migrating team cannot run both. Dual-name packages need the qualified form in files that `@changesets/cli` can no longer parse.\n    - Recommendation: state the compatibility claim precisely (\"npm-only workspaces round-trip; mixed workspaces use qualified keys\").\n\n12. **[consider] Competitor fit: scope against the field.**\n    - A team picks `changesets` for npm-only repos, `release-plz` for Cargo-only with conventional commits and registry diffing, `cargo-dist` for binary distribution and installers (Callisto only attaches binaries, with no installers or checksums yet), `semantic-release` for commit-driven single packages, and Nx release if it is already in the repo.\n    - Callisto wins on one narrow case: mixed Cargo+npm+PyPI repos that need a committed, auditable release roster. That case is real but small.\n    - Recommendation: lead the README with that case, and be explicit that there is no installer story. The repo already links competitors in docs/how-callisto-works.md; the 1.0 messaging should do the same.\n\n## Decisions that are right and should be defended\n\n- **ADR 2: the committed decision is the release authority.** CI verifies the merged diff against the committed decision and never re-derives cascade or group policy after merge. `fixed_group_cascade_bump_without_direct_changeset_is_accepted` and the tamper tests back it. It removes a whole class of \"the plan changed between review and publish\" bugs.\n- **ADR 3 and ADR 9: rerun by observing providers.** There is no execution journal, and an existing annotated tag on the planned commit is adopted whatever its message. State lives in the registries and Git, which is where it can be checked. Local and remote checks agree.\n- **ADR 4: native tools publish and own authentication.** Callisto never holds registry credentials in its own client code, and the secrets boundary is small and auditable.\n- **ADR 8 and invariant 2: format-preserving writes.** `toml_edit`, fingerprinted JSON, and `atomic_write` behind an `ApplyPermit` mean a version bump produces minimal diffs that reviewers can read.\n- **Directory-keyed internal identity (ADR 11).** The directory key is the right stable internal key and the display id is the wrong thing to persist. The mistake to avoid is letting the display id leak into tags (finding 1), not the key itself.\n\nSmaller observations: `init` writes a `callisto.toml` containing only a comment, which is the right amount of ceremony. The generated workflow is small. Unknown-key rejection and E316/E317 messages are clear and actionable.\n\nAll findings above are evidenced by file reads or the commands run. I did not find evidence that would let me rate the GitLab, Go or NuGet cases beyond the model-level coupling noted in finding 7."},"promptSource":"system","queueSkipSlashCommands":true,"turnOrigin":"peer","turnPosition":{"promptIndex":185,"turnIndex":327},"queueSkipAttachments":true,"sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.claude/worktrees/smoke-main","sessionId":"15c732d1-457b-4efc-91e5-664943f21b1c","version":"2.1.304","gitBranch":"chore/release-target-facts","slug":"lexical-jingling-puppy"}
