{"parentUuid":"c6f7bbda-1aee-4f1e-878a-558273bff449","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgMTo7DHyU3ixu1Ew6Xue","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"thinking","thinking":"","signature":""}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1626,"cache_read_input_tokens":581251,"output_tokens":5475,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1571},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1626,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":5475,"cache_read_input_tokens":581251,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1626,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1626},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"thinkingDurationMs":16357,"thinkingDisplay":"updates","serverClassifierRequest":"a10a4e30-9e45-4c3e-bbf3-b48c1604bd37","apiBlockIndex":0,"requestId":"req_011CgMTo6oz6jYz94KZQTsao","requestRef":"92caecef-2438-45ca-8ebc-bda817eb27f3","requestedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"37ba0ed5-51fa-4cfb-a0a8-2852b7f2553d","timestamp":"2026-10-24T17:33:25.969Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"15c732d1-457b-4efc-91e5-664943f21b1c","sessionKind":"bg","userType":"external","entrypoint":"cli","cwd":"/Users/gabe/Projects/callisto/.claude/worktrees/smoke-main","sessionId":"15c732d1-457b-4efc-91e5-664943f21b1c","version":"2.1.304","gitBranch":"chore/release-target-facts","slug":"lexical-jingling-puppy"}
{"parentUuid":"37ba0ed5-51fa-4cfb-a0a8-2852b7f2553d","isSidechain":false,"message":{"model":"claude-sonnet-5-5","id":"msg_011CgMTo7DHyU3ixu1Ew6Xue","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"# Adversarial review of Callisto 0.14.2\n\nFive independent reviews covered security, failure and recovery, versioning correctness, architecture and product fit, and test assurance. I personally reproduced or confirmed in code the six findings marked **Verified**. Everything else is the agents' evidence and is labelled as such.\n\n## Verdict\n\nThe release-safety core is sound. Effects are observed before they run, the committed decision is the release authority, and writes are format-preserving and atomic. The weak spots are mostly at the edges. A changeset with one bad entry re-bumps on every run. A half-finished release can be silently abandoned. The release commit is only loosely checked. Several 1.0 contracts are undefined or contradicted by their own docs. And the tests give false assurance in specific places.\n\n**Evidence labels:** Verified means I reproduced it or confirmed it in code. Reproduced means an agent ran it and showed output. Read means traced in code only. Several security attack paths are Read only, because that agent couldn't run anything.\n\n## Tier 1: fix before 1.0 (wrong results or unsafe)\n\n| # | Finding | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | A changeset with one unresolved entry (typo, removed package, quoted name) re-bumps its resolved packages on every run. A scratch repo went 1.0.0 → 1.1.0 → 1.2.0 with a duplicate changelog entry. The file stays on disk, so `release` would publish the extra versions. | **Verified** (reproduced by me) |\n| 2 | A local tag on an older commit marks a release finished. If the tag step succeeded locally but the push or GitHub work failed, and another commit lands, a rerun prints \"Nothing to release.\" The remote tag and GitHub release are never created. The code and `releasing.md` call this settled by design, but nothing warns about it. | Reproduced by agent; **code-confirmed** |\n| 3 | Release-commit verification doesn't limit which other files the release PR changes. It checks the decision, manifests, changelogs and a consumed changeset, but a hidden change to `.npmrc`, `.cargo/config.toml`, `build.rs`, `callisto.toml` or `.github/actions/**` passes. A branch named `callisto/version-packages` merges into a credentialed `execute`. | **Verified** (checks listed in code); attack Read |\n| 4 | Built-in registry destinations aren't bound by the intent. A crates.io or npm publish passes no explicit `--registry`, so a committed `.npmrc` or `.cargo/config.toml` decides where `NPM_TOKEN` or the cargo token goes. This contradicts the `binding.rs` doc. | **Verified** (argv code); exploit Read |\n| 5 | The `execute` job checks out `release-source` with `persist-credentials: true`, leaving a write-capable git token readable by `cargo publish` build scripts, npm lifecycle scripts and Python build backends. The same token force-pushes the `v1` floating tag. | **Verified** (workflow line); exploit Read |\n| 6 | A stable npm publish passes no `--tag`, so releasing a lower stable version (backport, late recovery) moves `latest` backwards. `releasing.md` admits the dist-tag isn't checked. | **Verified** (code) |\n\n## Tier 2: should fix (correct on common input, wrong on edges)\n\n| # | Finding | Evidence |\n|---|---|---|\n| 7 | A published, untagged version is orphaned when a later release PR bumps again. The rerun plans only the new version and E320 never fires. | Read |\n| 8 | E320's \"nothing moved\" check compares only the package directory. A change to the workspace root manifest, lockfile or a path-dependency sibling still lets the rerun tag a later commit. | Read |\n| 9 | A co-release raises peer ranges to the latest patch (`^1.0.0` becomes `^1.4.1`), which breaks consumers on 1.4.0. This matches VER-CAS-06 as written. | Reproduced |\n| 10 | npm ranges with spaces or a hyphen (`>=1.0.0 <2.0.0`) are silently ignored: no bump, no error. VER-CAS-06 says they should be E319. | Reproduced |\n| 11 | `package.json` rewrites go through `serde_json`, so large or `1.10` numbers change, inline arrays expand and escapes are lost. This conflicts with the format-preservation invariant. | Reproduced |\n| 12 | One member with no `version` (an xtask with `publish = false`) or a dynamic-version pyproject aborts the whole `version` run with E013. | Reproduced |\n| 13 | `apply` isn't transactional. If one manifest fails (inline `package = {…}`) after another was written, the tree is half-rewritten and the next run hits E121. | Reproduced |\n| 14 | Child-process credential scrubbing is a denylist of ten names. It misses `CARGO_REGISTRIES_*_TOKEN` (which the redactor already knows), `ACTIONS_ID_TOKEN_REQUEST_*` and `UV_PUBLISH_TOKEN`. `cargo publish` runs with its token and verify-builds dependencies. | Read |\n| 15 | With `version: latest`, the installer can fall back to building unreleased `main` HEAD. Attestation verification isn't tied to the release tag or source ref. | Read |\n| 16 | The generated matrix workflow runs `pip install twine` and `npx @napi-rs/cli` unlocked inside the credentialed execute job. | Read |\n| 17 | \"Already published\" is detected by bare substrings such as `e409`. A failing publish whose output contains it is treated as success, then waits six minutes and ends in E157 with the real error hidden. | Read |\n\n## Tier 3: contract and product (one-way doors)\n\n- **Tag names depend on workspace composition.** `foo@1.0.0` becomes `cargo/foo@1.1.0` when someone adds another package named `foo`. Tag names feed downloads, proto and users' scripts. This is the biggest 1.0 contract risk.\n- **ADR 12 still says \"Proposed\"** while the code and roadmap say canonical references shipped. Accepting it is your call.\n- **No stable machine contract.** Six release documents carry different `schemaVersion`s, shell `jq` readers have no version gate, and the hidden `release plan/execute`, `release-pr`, `matrix` and `schema` commands are public API that the workflow depends on.\n- **Exit codes** are only success or failure, so automation can't tell retry from stop. The same bad input gets E317 in one command and E227 in another, and an unknown config key reports E111 \"not valid TOML\".\n- **Dual-package ownership** is a hard-coded priority (Cargo, then npm, then PyPI), and a half-failed dual release has no named state in the docs.\n- **Legacy surface to delete before it becomes a promise:** the `[init]` table, `release.profiles`, the no-op `setup_git_user` input, and `nuget` as a `publish-to` value with no publisher.\n- **Onboarding:** a second uncommitted `version` run gives a misleading E121 \"interrupted run\". There's no first-publish guidance for crates.io, npm or PyPI, and generated workflows request `id-token: write` that nothing uses yet.\n- **Changeset compatibility** holds for npm-only repos. Mixed repos use `cargo/foo:` keys that `@changesets/cli` can't read, so the claim needs wording.\n- **Consumers get a weaker trust model than this repo does:** one job, no checksum step between jobs, and an unkeyed digest.\n\n## Tier 4: where the tests would not catch a regression\n\n- pnpm, yarn and bun publish paths are never run against the real tools.\n- The fake `gh`, `git`, `cargo` and `npm` rely on captured fixtures. `just provider-contract` is manual and not scheduled.\n- The generated workflow lint (actionlint, zizmor) silently skips in CI.\n- The 90% coverage gate is inflated by inline test code. Production-only coverage is about 89.9%, and the per-crate gate doesn't block.\n- `git_batch.rs`, a parser on the trust path, has no unit tests.\n- No automated link ties spec criteria to tests.\n- CI has no `timeout-minutes` or nextest slow-timeout.\n- The release-workflow contract test pins text lines, not behavior.\n- Fuzz targets are compiled but never run.\n\n## Smaller defects (Reproduced or Read)\n\n- A BOM'd changelog gets a duplicate H1 and a mid-file BOM.\n- Release notes are cut at the first indented `# ` line in a summary.\n- A BOM/CRLF workspace-root `Cargo.toml` is rewritten to LF without the BOM.\n- Poetry rewrites drop trailing comments, and Python `~=1.2` becomes `~=2.0.0` (narrower).\n- `snapshot --tag` with odd characters gives E038 \"internal error\".\n- `git add -A` in the release-PR script can commit stray untracked files.\n- The default branch name is substituted unquoted into YAML.\n- The recovery dispatch doesn't check the SHA is on `main`.\n- A queued `release-execute` run can be replaced by a newer one.\n- A concurrent run can leave a duplicate draft, and a half-uploaded asset wedges reruns.\n- An unsealed Python rerun can mix files from two builds.\n\n## What's done well, so don't touch it\n\n- The committed decision as authority, and execute re-deriving the intent from the detached checkout.\n- Observe-before-effect with `AbsentProof`, plus the simulator that injects crashes.\n- The confirmation wave and fail-closed typed observations.\n- The workflow policy script with mutation self-tests.\n- Format-preserving writes through `atomic_write` and `ApplyPermit`.\n- Argument-injection defences, artifact path and attestation handling, and the redaction tests.\n\n## Suggested order of work\n\n1. **Unresolved changeset entry** (#1): make it a hard error, or consume the file and strip only the bad entries. This is the smallest fix and the most embarrassing bug.\n2. **Release-commit allow-list** (#3), then **bind built-in registry destinations and stop persisting the git token** (#4, #5). These three move together and are the security PR.\n3. **Settled-tag recovery** (#2, #7, #8): a tag counts as settled only when the remote tag exists, and an untagged earlier version commit raises a diagnostic.\n4. **Dist-tag guard** (#6).\n5. **Range handling** (#9, #10) and the **two-phase apply** (#13).\n6. **1.0 contract pass:** decide tag naming, accept ADR 12, write the stability page, freeze exit codes, delete the legacy keys.\n7. **Assurance fixes:** make lint skips fail in CI, schedule `provider-contract`, add `git_batch` tests and CI timeouts.\n\n## Caveats\n\n- Only #1 was reproduced by me end to end, and #2 by an agent. The security attack paths were not run.\n- The agents don't know which CI jobs are required checks, since the ruleset isn't in the repo.\n- Agent scratch directories collided at times, so a few probes were redone elsewhere. Nothing was written to the repo.\n- I haven't triaged these against the open questions you already own, such as OIDC and the private-root exclusion.\n\nIf you want this as a shareable page, say so. Otherwise tell me which tier to start on; I'd begin with #1."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":"end_turn","stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1626,"cache_read_input_tokens":581251,"output_tokens":5475,"output_tokens_details":{"thinking_tokens":1571},"server_tool_use":{"web_search_requests":0,"web_fetch_requests":0},"service_tier":"standard","cache_creation":{"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1626,"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0},"inference_geo":"global","iterations":[{"input_tokens":2,"output_tokens":5475,"cache_read_input_tokens":581251,"cache_creation_input_tokens":1626,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":0,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":1626},"type":"message"}],"speed":"standard","fallback_credit":null},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"serverClassifierRequest":"a10a4e30-9e45-4c3e-bbf3-b48c1604bd37","apiBlockIndex":1,"requestId":"req_011CgMTo6oz6jYz94KZQTsao","requestRef":"92caecef-2438-45ca-8ebc-bda817eb27f3","requestedModel":"claude-sonnet-5-5","type":"assistant","uuid":"49328b04-8b01-4a88-b925-fbdaeb2d844b","timestamp":"2026-10-24T17:33:46.726Z","advisorModel":"claude-opus-5-5","effort":"medium","perTurnEffort":"medium","session_id":"15c732d1-457b-4efc-91e5-664943f21b